WEEK–2: Single-Node Kafka — Create Topic, Produce & Consume Messages
🎯 Aim

To demonstrate a single-node, single-broker Kafka cluster and perform basic operations such as:

Start Kafka and ZooKeeper
Create a Kafka topic
Describe the topic
Produce messages
Consume messages
STEP 0: Start Docker

Open Ubuntu Terminal:

sudo systemctl start docker

Check:

docker ps
STEP 1: Start ZooKeeper

If you already completed Week-1 and the containers are still present, don't create them again.

First check:

docker ps -a
If zookeeper and kafka already exist

Start them:

docker start zookeeper
docker start kafka

Then check:

docker ps

You should see:

zookeeper
kafka
If they don't exist

Run:

docker run -d --name zookeeper -p 2181:2181 wurstmeister/zookeeper

Then:

docker run -d --name kafka -p 9092:9092 --link zookeeper:zookeeper -e KAFKA_ZOOKEEPER_CONNECT=zookeeper:2181 -e KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS=PLAINTEXT://localhost:9092 -e KAFKA_LISTENERS=PLAINTEXT://0.0.0.0:9092 wurstmeister/kafka

These are the same Kafka/ZooKeeper containers used in your Week-1 setup.

STEP 2: Check Kafka is Running

Run:

docker ps

You should see both:

zookeeper
kafka
If Kafka isn't running

Check:

docker logs kafka
STEP 3: Open a Shell Inside Kafka Container

This is the easiest way to run Kafka commands.

docker exec -it kafka bash

Your terminal prompt will change to something similar to:

bash-4.2#

Now you are inside the Kafka container.

STEP 4: Create a Topic

Your document uses the topic name:

quickstart-events

Run:

bin/kafka-topics.sh --create --topic quickstart-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

You should get something like:

Created topic quickstart-events.

✅ Topic created.

STEP 5: Describe the Topic

Now run:

bin/kafka-topics.sh --describe --topic quickstart-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

Your document shows output containing:

PartitionCount: 1
ReplicationFactor: 1

and:

Partition: 0
Leader: 0
Replicas: 0
Isr: 0

What this means
PartitionCount = 1 → topic has one partition
ReplicationFactor = 1 → one copy of the data
Leader = 0 → broker 0 is the leader

This confirms it is a single-broker setup.

STEP 6: Start the Kafka Producer

Still inside the Kafka container, run:

bin/kafka-console-producer.sh --topic quickstart-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

This is the producer command from your document.

You will see:

>

Now type messages.

For example:

Hello Kafka

Press Enter.

Then:

This is my first message

Press Enter.

Then:

This is my second message

Press Enter.

So it looks like:

>Hello Kafka
>This is my first message
>This is my second message
⚠️ Don't close this terminal yet.

Keep the producer running.

STEP 7: Open a SECOND Terminal

This is important.

Open another Ubuntu Terminal.

Keep Terminal 1 running.

In Terminal 2, enter the Kafka container:

docker exec -it kafka bash
STEP 8: Start the Kafka Consumer

In Terminal 2, run:

bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic quickstart-events --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

This is the consumer command from your lab material.

You should see:

Hello Kafka
This is my first message
This is my second message

🎉 Kafka Producer → Kafka Topic → Kafka Consumer is working.

